 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087700 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275251 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087700 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275252 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087700 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275253 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087700 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186339006 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087700 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186339007 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087700 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348160 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087700 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348161 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087700 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348162 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087700 #edb/ref 1039 "Determining the Inverse of a Larger Matrix" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087700 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186087507 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087700 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "a75c67e5-3469-5730-9181-54df4ec6d5b2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087930 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186088524 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087930 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186088525 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087930 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186088526 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087930 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186088527 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087930 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087930 #edb/ref 1045 "Introduction to the Inverse of a Matrix" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087930 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186088524 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087930 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049125 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087930 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "e07e7ccb-9045-51ac-ad31-1220ff860794" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088524 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088525 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088524 #edb/ref 1061 16452 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088524 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "390345b1-ff60-5803-bf48-ae86115b2de2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088524 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186088893 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088525 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088526 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088525 #edb/ref 1061 16453 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088525 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "42dd802f-22c6-5586-8fae-028b66defc18" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088525 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186087699 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088526 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088527 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088526 #edb/ref 1061 16454 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088526 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3941120f-c21f-51b0-b616-5b3059c55fc7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088526 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186087700 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088527 #edb/ref 1061 16456 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088527 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "46575a16-9bf0-5f72-8c33-a6d4bf578d86" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088527 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186088894 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088893 #edb/ref 1064 "Given the scalar $a,$ the **multiplicative inverse** of $a$ is defined as the scalar $a^{-1}$ such that\n\n$$\na \\cdot a^{-1} = a^{-1} \\cdot a = 1.\n$$\n\nFor example, the multiplicative inverse of $2$ is the number $2^{-1} = \\dfrac{1}{2}$ because \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}\\cdot 2 =  2\\cdot \\dfrac{1}{2} = 1.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, given a square $n \\times n$ matrix $A,$ the **inverse** of $A$ is defined as the $n \\times n$ matrix $A^{-1}$ such that\n\n$$\nA \\cdot A^{-1} = A^{-1} \\cdot A = I_n,\n$$\n\nwhere $I_n$ is the $n\\times n$ identity matrix.\n\nFor example, suppose we're given the following $2\\times 2$ matrix:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe inverse of the above matrix is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo verify that the above matrix is in fact the inverse of $A,$ first observe that if we multiply $A$ by the above matrix $A^{-1},$ then we get the identity matrix $I_2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}{{[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{A}^{-1}} & =[\\begin{aligned}-1\\cdot (-2)+(-1)\\cdot 1 & -1\\cdot (-1)+(-1)\\cdot 1 \\\\ 1\\cdot (-2)+2\\cdot 1 & 1\\cdot (-1)+2\\cdot 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={I}_{2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLikewise, if we multiply $A^{-1}$ by $A,$ then we also get the identity matrix $I_2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]{{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A} & ={I}_{2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe do not yet know how to find the inverse of a matrix. However, we can verify that one matrix is the inverse of the other by carrying out the same procedure as we did here." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088893 #edb/ref 1065 3043 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088893 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "427bce08-8aa6-508f-969b-2a2ed4eb8c7a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088893 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088894 #edb/ref 1064 "The number $0$ does not have an inverse for multiplication over the real numbers, because there's no such number $a$ that $a \\cdot 0 = 1.$ Instead, for every $a$ we have $a \\cdot 0 = 0.$\n\nSimilarly, not every matrix has an inverse.\n\n- If a matrix is not square, an inverse does not exist.\n\n- Some square matrices do not have an inverse. If a square matrix does not have an inverse, then it is said to be **singular**. An example of a singular matrix is the zero matrix\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe will soon learn how to calculate the inverse of a square matrix, and we'll also soon learn how to determine when a square matrix does not have an inverse." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088894 #edb/ref 1065 3044 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088894 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "fea084a4-999a-5a17-8f35-e8d7e5dbf25c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088894 #edb/ref 1067 "Not Every Matrix Has an Inverse" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186235811 #edb/ref 1080 "None" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186235811 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186235811 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "02baf16c-a8a2-53b2-8bea-aa19d8808d8d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186236523 #edb/ref 1080 "II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186236523 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186236523 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "6fc2854a-e4b2-5324-9a08-e2af2e4b123a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186236534 #edb/ref 1080 "II and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186236534 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186236534 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "70cccdb8-622d-5f6a-a9ea-eccb505a5ce9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237100 #edb/ref 1080 "I only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237100 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237100 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "ca25bc48-8a11-55ab-ac29-64db2542d857" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237254 #edb/ref 1080 "I, II, and III" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237254 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237254 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e47abf58-d25c-5045-94d3-e001e656f0b0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237657 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186235811 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237657 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186236523 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237657 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186236534 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237657 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186237100 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237657 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186237254 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237657 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237657 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237657 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186237100 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237657 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "a75c28b7-5aa7-50f4-afc3-613425ae1089" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237917 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237657 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237917 #edb/ref 1069 "q-102666" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237917 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237917 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the matrices\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} & -\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $AB=BA={I}_{2}$\n\nII. $AC=CA={I}_{2}$\n\nIII. $C={A}^{-1}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237917 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "efd9ac2b-7a32-57f6-9899-3da1aeffaa13" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237917 #edb/ref 1074 "- First, we check whether $AB=BA={I}_{2}.$ Computing $AB$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} & -\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={I}_{2}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLikewise, computing $BA$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BA & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} & -\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={I}_{2}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, statement I is true.\n\n- Now, we check whether $AC=CA={I}_{2}.$ Computing $AC$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AC & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne {I}_{2}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, statement II is false. As a result, $C\\ne {A}^{-1}$ and statement III is false too.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345824 #edb/ref 1080 "I and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345824 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045